- Split by deck identity. A random snapshot split gives deceptively optimistic validation because the same 60-card list appears repeatedly.
- Keep the expected-count vector as the primary belief. It can interpolate between known archetypes and represent unseen variants.
- Apply hard evidence only when producing concrete samples. A sample cannot contradict an already revealed card count.
- Do not force the prediction onto a catalog of known lists. One new card can define a legitimate unseen hybrid.
- Report accuracy by information level. Performance with one revealed card and performance with twenty revealed cards answer different questions.

